Continue from the previous stage. Work in /work and do not commit changes.

Apply terrain movement speed consistently to all unit movement.

Required behavior:

- mountain tiles multiply movement speed by 0.5;
- forest tiles multiply movement speed by 0.75;
- path tiles multiply movement speed by 2.0;
- plains tiles multiply movement speed by 1.0.

Requirements:

1. Add a small reusable helper that safely resolves the terrain cell and speed
   multiplier for an in-bounds world position.
2. Apply the multiplier to actual distance traveled, not only to animation or
   A* scoring.
3. Apply it in every movement route: direct orders, cached-waypoint movement,
   combat pursuit, autonomous king/knight movement, return-to-post behavior,
   AI movement, and local-separation displacement where appropriate.
4. Preserve frame-rate independence. A unit crossing a terrain boundary during
   a large update must not gain an obviously incorrect full-frame bonus from
   the tile it started on. Subdivide or clamp integration at tile boundaries if
   needed, while keeping the common case efficient.
5. Preserve existing map clamping, guard leashes, formation settling, attack
   ranges, navigation invalidation, and collision behavior.
6. Terrain must not change base Unit.speed values. Compute effective speed at
   movement time so stats, combat assessment, and saved expectations retain
   their original meaning.
7. A stationary unit must remain stationary on every terrain kind.
8. Visual variation indices must have no effect on speed.
9. Do not make mountains impassable; they are traversable at half speed.
10. Decide explicitly whether separation uses the current tile multiplier and
    document the choice. Prefer bounding separation so path tiles cannot create
    tunneling or excessive overlap correction.

Add deterministic tests proving, for the same unit and elapsed time on an open
map:

- mountain distance is 0.5 times plains distance;
- forest distance is 0.75 times plains distance;
- path distance is 2.0 times plains distance;
- plains retains the prior baseline distance;
- results are approximately equal across 30, 60, and 120 FPS updates;
- crossing a tile boundary produces a stable, correctly split distance;
- diagonal movement uses the same scalar multiplier without becoming faster
  than cardinal movement;
- every unit kind receives the multiplier;
- variation 0, 1, 2, and 3 produce identical movement for the same kind;
- map-edge clamping and guard-leash limits still hold;
- issuing a new order and following cached waypoints both respect terrain.

